      chat.complete tokens=512 promptTokens=8451 kvReused=0 kvReusePercent=0.0 ttftMs=34044 elapsedMs=85669.0 tokensPerSec=5.98 finishReason=max_tokens assistantOutput="\n\n# Hash Tables: A Comprehensive Deep Dive\n\nHash tables are one of the most fundamental and widely used data structures in computer science. They provide a way to store and retrieve data using a key-value mapping, offering average-case constant time complexity for insertion, deletion, and lookup operations. This efficiency makes them indispensable in applications ranging from database indexing and caching systems to compiler symbol tables and network routing.\n\nHowever, the simplicity of the concept belies the complexity of its implementation. To truly understand hash tables, one must grasp the underlying mechanics of hashing functions, collision resolution strategies, dynamic resizing, and the mathematical principles governing their performance. This explanation will dissect these components in detail, providing a theoretical framework and a practical worked example.\n\n## 1. The Core Concept: Mapping Keys to Indices\n\nAt its heart, a hash table is an array of buckets (or slots). The goal is to map a key (which can be a string, integer, object, etc.) to a specific index in this array. This mapping is performed by a **hash function**.\n\n### The Hash Function\nA hash function $h(k)$ takes a key $k$ and returns an integer. Ideally, this integer should be uniformly distributed across the range of possible indices. In practice, we often use a modulo operation to constrain the hash value to the size of the array $m$:\n\n$$ \text{index} = h(k) \pmod m $$\n\nFor example, if we have an array of size $m=10$, and our hash function returns the integer 42, the index would be $42 \pmod{10} = 2$. The key would be stored in bucket 2.\n\n### Why Hashing is Fast\nThe speed of a hash table comes from the fact that calculating the hash value and performing the modulo operation are constant-time operations ($O(1)$). Once the index is calculated, accessing the array at that index is also $O(1)$. Therefore, if we could guarantee that every key maps to a unique index, we would have a perfect hash table with $O(1)$ performance for all operations.\n\n## 2. The Problem: Collisions\n\nIn reality, perfect hashing is rarely possible for dynamic datasets. Two different keys might produce the same hash value (or the same index after modulo). This is known as a **collision**.\n\nFor example:\n- Key "Alice" hashes to index 5.\n- Key "Bob" also hashes to index"
